        "reason-1": {
          "type": "reason",
          "settings": {
            "number": "1",
            "title": "Your baby's bottle could be releasing millions of microplastic particles into every single feeding.",
            "body": "<p>In 2020, researchers from Trinity College Dublin published a study in <em>Nature Food</em> examining polypropylene, the plastic used in most baby bottles. After following the WHO’s standard formula-preparation guidelines, they found that a single bottle could release up to 16 million microplastic particles per liter, along with trillions of much smaller nanoplastic particles. The researchers estimated that a bottle-fed baby could ingest more than one million of these particles every day. Since polypropylene accounts for around 82% of baby bottles sold worldwide, millions of parents may be exposing their babies without ever being warned.</p>"
          }
        },
        "image-2": {
          "type": "image",
          "settings": {
            "image": "https://mokababy.co/cdn/shop/t/12/assets/moka-listicle-bloodstream-particles-4x3.jpg?v=181696134951427253691783477672",
            "alt": "Microscopic particles moving among red blood cells",
            "caption": "Illustration of microscopic particles moving among red blood cells"
          }
        },
        "reason-2": {
          "type": "reason",
          "settings": {
            "number": "2",
            "title": "Microscopic doesn't mean harmless. It means it gets everywhere.",
            "body": "<p>What most parents are never told is that these particles may not simply pass through the body. Some are small enough to move beyond the gut, and microplastics have already been detected in human blood, breast milk, and placental tissue. Because an infant’s organs and immune system are still developing, researchers are especially concerned about repeated exposure during the earliest and most vulnerable months of life. Studies continue to investigate possible links to inflammation, oxidative stress, and hormone disruption while the brain, organs, and endocrine system are still forming. Tiny was never the same as harmless.</p>"
          }
        },
        "image-3": {
          "type": "image",
          "settings": {
            "image": "https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0979/5082/6776/files/ChatGPT_Image_Jul_13_2026_03_47_16_PM_800x800.png?v=1783932697",
            "alt": "Bottle sterilizing and scratched plastic placeholder",
            "caption": "Macro view of scratches inside a repeatedly washed glass bottle"
          }
        },
        "reason-3": {
          "type": "reason",
          "settings": {
            "number": "3",
            "title": "It’s not a one-time problem. Every wash and every round of sterilization makes it worse.",
            "body": "<p>The same study found that the problem builds with every use. Heat and friction are what cause polypropylene to break down, and both are part of nearly every feeding routine: hot water, sterilizing, shaking, and scrubbing. Each cycle can wear down the bottle’s surface and release even more particles. When the water temperature increased from the recommended 158°F to near boiling, particle release rose from roughly 6 million to 55 million per liter. In other words, the bottle you sterilize to make safer may be shedding more plastic because of the very process meant to keep it clean.</p>"
          }
        },
