        "reason-5": {
          "type": "reason",
          "settings": {
            "number": "5",
            "title": "Even most “glass” bottles still let polypropylene touch your baby’s milk.",
            "body": "<p>Switching to glass may seem like the obvious solution—until you examine how many popular bottles are actually built. Brands such as Dr. Brown’s, Philips Avent, and Comotomo may use a genuine glass body, while collars, vents, and other parts exposed to warm milk are still often made from polypropylene—the same plastic examined in the study. Lawsuits filed in 2024 have accused several major brands, including Philips Avent and Dr. Brown’s, of failing to warn parents about microplastic release when polypropylene bottles are heated. Nuk and Tommee Tippee have faced similar allegations. A bottle labeled “glass” can still place plastic exactly where it matters most.</p>"
          }
        },
